t p_{\\text{target}} We examine how p target p_{\\text{target}} varies across tokens that execute different latent CoT lengths. The results in Figure 8 exhibit a clear monotonic trend: tokens with longer latent CoT tend to have lower p target p_{\\text{target}} , whereas tokens with higher p target p_{\\text{target}} typically halt earlier. This observation aligns with our correctness-aware design: pruning is encouraged when the ground-truth token already receives a high probability, while ambiguous tokens are allocated more latent computation. 4.4.2 Token Difficulty vs. Latent CoT Length On WikiText ( Merity et al., 2016 ) , we use token-wise cross-entropy as a difficulty score and bucket tokens accordingly. As shown in Figure 8 , latent CoT length increases monotonically with difficulty: easy (low-loss) tokens execute ∼ \\sim 0–1 latent steps on average, while difficult (high-loss) tokens trigger deeper computation and approach the maximum length. This suggests the model learns a sensible compute-allocation rule, using minimal latent CoT for easy tokens and longer latent CoT for difficult ones. Figure 8 : Left: p target p_{\\text{target}} monotonically decreases with latent CoT length: tokens that generate longer latent CoT have lower p target p_{\\text{target}} . Right: We bucket tokens by token-wise cross-entropy (log-scale) and report average latent COT length for 1.4B models with l max ∈ { 3 , 5 } l_{\\max}\\in\\{3,5\\} . Easy tokens use ∼ \\sim 0–1 steps, while harder tokens receive more computation. 4.4.3 Case Studies Finally, we visualize where the model chooses to generate long latent CoT in real sequences. Figure 7 shows two examples from the 1.4B model with max latent CoT length ℓ max = 5 \\ell_{\\max}=5 , where each token is colored by the number of executed latent steps. The router assigns minimal computation to highly predictable spans (e.g., common function words and locally determined continuations), while allocating longer latent CoT to information-dense tokens such as entities, numbers, and key content words. 5 Ablation Studies We run controlled ablations on 70M-parameter Llama models to isolate key design choices in our adaptive latent CoT. 5.1 Effect of Router Architecture. We compare five router designs with the backbone and training setup fixed ( Table 2 ). No-Adaptive disables routing (no pruning). Linear / MLP share one router across latent steps, while Multi-Linear / Multi-MLP use step-specific routers (no sharing). As shown in Table 2 , adaptive routing improves efficiency while also improving performance. The shared Linear router achieves the lowest CE loss, and we use it as the default. Table 2 : Effect of router architecture ( l max = 5 l_{\\max}=5 ). No-Adaptive disables routing (no pruning). Linear / MLP share one router across latent steps, while Multi-Linear / Multi-MLP use step-specific routers. We report CE loss (lower is better) and prune ratio (higher saves more compute). Notably, shared Linear outperforms No-Adaptive in CE loss while pruning ∼ \\sim 12% of steps. Router λ \\lambda β \\beta CE ↓ \\downarrow Prune Ratio (%) ↑ \\uparrow No-Adaptive – – 2.671 – MLP 0.4 10 2.675 13.4 Linear 0.4 10 2.670 12.4 Multi-MLP 0.4 10 2.672 13.8 Multi-Linear 0.4 10 2.672 11.8 5.2 Effect of λ \\lambda and β \\beta in the Adaptive Loss. We ablate the two key hyperparameters in our correctness-aware adaptive loss ( Equation 10 ): the compute weight λ \\lambda and the exponent β \\beta on p target p_{\\text{target}} . All runs use a shared linear router with l max = 5 l_{\\max}{=}5 ; when varying λ \\lambda we fix β = 10 \\beta{=}10 , and when varying β \\beta we fix λ = 0.4 \\lambda{=}0.4 . As shown in Figure 9 (a), larger λ \\lambda increases the prune ratio but slightly worsens CE loss, reflecting a quality–efficiency trade-off; meanwhile Figure 9 (b) shows that larger β \\beta improves CE loss but reduces pruning, indicating more conservative early exits focused on highly confident tokens. We adopt λ = 0.4 \\lambda{=}0.4 and β = 10 \\beta{=}10 in our experiments. Figure 9 : Ablations on loss hyperparameters and max latent cot length l max l_{\\max} . Blue curves (left axis) report CE loss (lower is better), and red curves (right axis) report prune ratio (higher indicates more pruning), defined as 1 − 𝔼 ⁡ [ ℓ ] l max 1-\\frac{\\mathbb{E}[\\ell]}{l_{\\max}} , where ℓ \\ell denotes the executed latent CoT length per token. (a) Vary λ \\lambda with β = 10 \\beta{=}10 and l max = 5 l_{\\max}{=}5 . (b) Vary β \\beta with λ = 0.4 \\lambda{=}0.4 and l max = 5 l_{\\max}{=}5 . (c) Vary l max l_{\\max} with λ = 0.4 \\lambda{=}0.4 and β = 10 \\beta{=}10 . 5.3 Effect of max latent CoT length l max l_{\\max} . We vary l max l_{\\max} and evaluate its impact on CE loss and pruning. As shown in Figure 9 (c), larger l max l_{\\max} consistently reduces CE loss, suggesting extra latent cot improves modeling quality. Meanwhile, the prune ratio also increases, indicating the router uses the larger budget to skip more steps on easy tokens while allocating computation to difficult ones. 6 Conclusion We introduced Adaptive Latent COT, a one-stage pretraining framework that increases per-token computation by generating variable-length latent COT before emitting each token. Experiments on LLaMA backbones show consistent improvements in language-modeling perplexity and downstream tasks compared to strong compute-scaling baselines. Acknowledgement This work is sponsored by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (NSFC) grant (No. 62576211) and the National Key Research and Development Program of China (No. 2023ZD0121402). References Bae et al. (2025) S. Bae, Y. Kim, R. Bayat, S. Kim, J. Ha, T. Schuster, A. Fisch, H. Harutyunyan, Z. Ji, A. Courville, et al. Mixture-of-recursions: learning dynamic recursive depths for adaptive token-level computation . arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.10524 . Cited by: §1 , §2 , §4.1 . Banino et al. (2021) A. Banino, J. Balaguer, and C. Blundell Pondernet: 