u [ 21 ] , we first describe the analytical foundation of our work: self-attention can be interpreted as a form of mean-shift clustering. Based on this perspective, we then explore the among-query properties of self-attention and leveraging the formal definition of TSA (as introduced in Definition 3.1 ) to construct and complete our theoretical proof. -D 1 Self-Attention as Mean-Shift Clustering Implicit Clustering in Attention The softmax weighting means that the query 𝐪 \\mathbf{q} mainly focuses on a subset of keys with the largest dot-products 𝐪 ⋅ 𝐤 j \\mathbf{q}\\cdot\\mathbf{k}_{j} . This behavior causes tokens to form clusters: tokens (keys) that are similar to a given query will receive high attention weights (“cluster members”), whereas unrelated tokens get negligible weight. In other words, each query picks out clusters of relevant key tokens and largely ignores the rest, which is exactly the token sparsity phenomenon [ 49 , 30 ] (only a few tokens significantly contribute to the attention result). Mean-Shift Interpretation Importantly, the Deja Vu study observed that self-attention implements a one-step mean-shift clustering update . Formally, fixing within one head, for the attention output 𝐲 = ∑ i A i ​ ( 𝐱 i ​ 𝐖 V ) \\mathbf{y}{=}\\sum_{i}A_{i}(\\mathbf{x}_{i}\\mathbf{W}_{V}) , if we ignore the 𝐖 V \\mathbf{W}_{V} projection for a moment, this computes the weighted centroid of the token embeddings 𝐱 \\mathbf{x} around the query 𝐪 \\mathbf{q} (in the projected space). That is, define an attention mean m ⁡ ( 𝐪 ) m(\\mathbf{q}) as the weighted mean of the embeddings: m ⁡ ( q ) = ∑ i 𝐊 ⁡ ( 𝐱 i , 𝐪 ) ​ 𝐱 i ∑ i 𝐊 ⁡ ( 𝐱 i , 𝐪 ) , m(q)\\;=\\;\\frac{\\sum_{i}\\mathbf{K}(\\mathbf{x}_{i},\\mathbf{q})\\,\\mathbf{x}_{i}}{\\sum_{i}\\mathbf{K}(\\mathbf{x}_{i},\\mathbf{q})}\\,, (35) so 𝐲 = m ⁡ ( 𝐪 ) ​ 𝐖 V \\mathbf{y}{=}m(\\mathbf{q})\\mathbf{W}_{V} . This is exactly the update step of the mean-shift clustering algorithm : m ⁡ ( 𝐪 ) m(\\mathbf{q}) is pulling the query’s embedding toward a nearby cluster of points 𝐱 i {\\mathbf{x}_{i}} that are similar to 𝐪 \\mathbf{q} . In mean-shift, one would iteratively set 𝐪 ← m ⁡ ( 𝐪 ) \\mathbf{q}\\leftarrow m(\\mathbf{q}) to converge to the cluster center (mode of the density). In a Transformer, we usually apply just one such step per layer, but the analogy holds: each attention head nudges the query vector toward the centroid of a cluster of key vectors . -D 2 Among-Query Property Problem Setting Consider two queries 𝐪 t \\mathbf{q}_{t} and 𝐪 t + 1 \\mathbf{q}_{t+1} from adjacent time steps in a sequence. Thanks to residual connections and the smooth nature of transformer dynamics, adjacent queries in a sequence tend to be close in embedding space [ 28 , 42 ] . We then assume as a general principle that 𝐪 t + 1 \\mathbf{q}_{t+1} is a small perturbation of 𝐪 t \\mathbf{q}_{t} (i.e. 𝐪 t + 1 ≈ 𝐪 t \\mathbf{q}_{t+1}\\approx\\mathbf{q}_{t} in ℓ 2 \\ell_{2} norm or cosine similarity), as the among-query property only concerns the behavior of attention when queries are similar, we revise the Among-query Property 1 that: Property 2 (Among-query Property) . If two query vectors 𝐪 t \\mathbf{q}_{t} and 𝐪 t + 1 \\mathbf{q}_{t+1} are aligned enough in vector space, then the distributions of their attention weights are also very similar. In other words, adjacent queries attend to largely the same cluster of key tokens. As 𝐪 t \\mathbf{q}_{t} shifts to 𝐪 t + 1 \\mathbf{q}_{t+1} , the attention weight mass simply shifts smoothly among those keys–obeying a continuous mean-shift-like change–rather than jumping to a completely different set of keys. Intuition via Mean-Shift Think of the keys 𝐤 i {\\mathbf{k}_{i}} as data points in the embedding space. The query 𝐪 t \\mathbf{q}_{t} will put most weight on keys in some neighborhood (a “cluster”) around 𝐪 t \\mathbf{q}_{t} . Now if 𝐪 t + 1 \\mathbf{q}_{t+1} moves slightly (say 𝐪 t + 1 = 𝐪 t + Δ \\mathbf{q}_{t+1}=\\mathbf{q}_{t}+\\Delta for a small Δ \\Delta ),